  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM MDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge is parked over Colorado today, with east to\nnortheast flow steering some limited, recycled moisture around. \nWe are already seeing some convection firing up in the higher \nterrain of the Gila Region and Sacramento Mountains. Much like \nyesterday, these storms will drift into the lowlands, but struggle\nas they move into a more hostile environment. The 18Z sounding \ntries to look impressive, with 1500 J/Kg and limited CIN based on \nthe forecast surface temperature, but this doesn't take into \naccount mixing out of the dewpoints, which are on their way to the\nlower-40s for the lowlands. \n\nDrier and warmer air aloft will work into the area through the \nweekend, heavily suppressing convection chances, even in the \nhigher terrain. Temperatures will remain in the 100 to 103 range \nin the lowlands through the weekend, and will attempt to crawl a \nlittle higher than that early next week as the upper ridge settles\nover southern New Mexico. Very spotty convection may return to \nthe higher terrain around that time.",
